  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Sunday...\n\nMorning light showers/sprinkles have exited southeast MT and dry \nconditions exist across the forecast area. Most of the area will \nremain dry today, but cannot rule out a t-storm or two near the \nDakotas border in the late afternoon and early evening, though \nadmittedly the greater chance of this is in the Dakotas. \nMesoanalysis shows a 850mb dewpt tongue of >+10C over southeast \nMT, so instability will build with daytime heating. The question \nis whether or not the air mass remains capped. Low confidence, but\nthe mention of isolated TS is warranted. Mixed NW-N winds will \nbecome somewhat breezy over the western two-thirds of our forecast\narea this afternoon, which along with RHs in the low-mid teens \nwill keep fire weather conditions elevated through the day. Be \ncareful to not spark a fire. Wildfire smoke will continue to \nadvect from the west for the foreseeable future, keeping skies \nquite hazy, with occasionally reduced surface visibilities and air\nquality. Unfortunately there is no end in sight for the smoke \nissues.\n\nLow level easterly winds will continue to push up pwats the next\ncouple of days, especially over the east. There is a low chance\n(10-20%) of late day t-storms in the far east each of the next two\ndays. Forcing will be weak and it is also possible that the air\nmass will remain too capped for storms to develop. Smoke cover\nwill also be a hindrance.\n\nPwats eventually become 1-2 standard deviations above normal by\nmidweek. Along with falling heights from a trof dropping out of\nsouthwest Canada, this will lead to increasing shower and\nthunderstorm potential from Wednesday through the end of the week.\nEnsembles show pwats increasing to greater than an inch close to\nthe western foothills by Wednesday night. Lightning from t-storm\nactivity may be problematic with regard to fire potential after\nthe current stretch of hot & dry days (most of our cwa has not\nseen measurable rain yet this month, and won't for the next few \ndays), but the storms will no doubt be wet and could produce heavy\nrain as well as gusty surface winds. Timing of waves and periods \nof ascent/subsidence into late week are difficult to determine \nnow, but generally speaking the atmospheric moisture will remain \nelevated and the risk of wet t-storms will continue daily through \nthe weekend.\n\nTemperatures will transition from above normal early in the week \nto below normal at the end. Look for upper 80s to mid 90s Monday \nand Tuesday, followed by 80s Wednesday, then 70s to lower 80s \nThursday through the weekend.\n\nAs a side note, there is a tropical wave near 10N 140W that is\nlikely to become a named storm southeast of Hawaii over the next 7\ndays. Models at this time have varying solutions with the \nevolution of this wave, but it may eventually impact the longwave \npattern over the central and eastern Pacific during the latter \npart of August, and is worth watching. We are in the time of year\nwhen storms in the tropical Pacific can alter large scale weather\npatterns.\n\nJKL",
